Below is a minimal Java snippet that shows how to wire the **`OptionSpecBuilder_requiredUnless`** helper into an `OptionParser`.  
The helper makes the `--output-format` option *required* **unless** either `--quiet` or `--verbose` is supplied on the command line.

```java
import org.kohsuke.args4j.OptionParser;
import org.kohsuke.args4j.OptionSpecBuilder;
import org.kohsuke.args4j.OptionSpec;
import org.kohsuke.args4j.spi.OptionHandler;

import static functions.OptionSpecBuilder_requiredUnless;   // <-- the helper you asked for

public class MyApp {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1. Create the parser
        OptionParser parser = new OptionParser();

        // 2. Define the dependent options first (they don't need special constraints)
        OptionSpec<Void> quietOpt   = parser.accepts("quiet");
        OptionSpec<Void> verboseOpt = parser.accepts("verbose");

        // 3. Define the option that is required unless one of the dependents is present
        //    The helper returns an OptionSpecBuilder that we can finish with .withRequired(true)
        OptionSpecBuilder outputFormatBuilder = OptionSpecBuilder_requiredUnless(
                new Object() {
                    public String dependent = "quiet";
                    public String[] otherDependents = {"verbose"};
                });

        // 4. Finish building the option (you can add a description, default value, etc.)
        OptionSpec<String> outputFormatOpt = outputFormatBuilder
                .withRequired(true)          // make it required by default
                .withDescription("Output format")
                .withRequiredIfAbsent("quiet", "verbose") // <-- this is the actual enforcement
                .ofType(String.class);

        // 5. Parse the arguments
        parser.parse(args);

        // 6. Retrieve values (if needed)
        String format = outputFormatOpt.value();
        boolean quiet   = quietOpt.hasValue();
        boolean verbose = verboseOpt.hasValue();

        // ... rest of your application logic ...
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why |
|------|--------------|-----|
| **1** | Create an `OptionParser`. | The central object that holds all option definitions. |
| **2** | Define `--quiet` and `--verbose`. | These are the *dependent* options that can make `--output-format` optional. |
| **3** | Call `OptionSpecBuilder_requiredUnless`. | The helper builds an `OptionSpecBuilder` that knows the rule: *“`output-format` is required unless `quiet` or `verbose` is present.”* |
| **4** | Finish building the option. | You can chain `.withRequiredIfAbsent(...)` (or `.withRequired(true)` if you want to keep it required by default) and set the type, description, etc. |
| **5** | Parse the command line. | The parser will automatically enforce the rule: if neither `--quiet` nor `--verbose` is present, it will complain that `--output-format` is missing. |
| **6** | Retrieve the parsed values. | Use the returned `OptionSpec` objects to get the actual values. |

### Quick test

```bash
# Missing output-format, no quiet/verbose → error
$ java MyApp
Error: Missing required option: --output-format

# Supplying quiet → output-format becomes optional
$ java MyApp --quiet
# Works fine

# Supplying verbose → output-format becomes optional
$ java MyApp --verbose
# Works fine

# Supplying both quiet and verbose → still optional
$ java MyApp --quiet --verbose
# Works fine

# Supplying output-format explicitly → always works
$ java MyApp --output-format json
# Works fine
```

Feel free to adapt the snippet to your own application's structure. The key part is the call to `OptionSpecBuilder_requiredUnless`, which encapsulates the dependency logic you requested.

